.\" qt6sermo.1 — man page for qt6sermo @PACKAGE_VERSION@
.\" haplo-dialog <devel@haplo-dialog.fr>
.\" This file is installed as /usr/share/man/man1/qt6sermo.1
.\"
.TH QT6SERMO 1 "October 2026" "sermo @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "sermo User Commands"
.SH NAME
qt6sermo \- Qt6 dialog box creator for shell scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qt6sermo
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.br
.B qt6sermo
\fB\-\-program\fR=\fIVARIABLE\fR
.br
.B qt6sermo
\fB\-\-file\fR=\fIFILE\fR
.br
.B qt6sermo
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
.br
.IR VARIABLE =\(aq<window>...</window>\(aq
.B qt6sermo
\fB\-\-program\fR=\fIVARIABLE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qt6sermo
reads a Qt6 GUI description written in an XML dialect and displays
the resulting dialog window.
It is designed to be called from shell scripts to present interactive forms,
progress bars, list views, and other widgets without requiring knowledge of
Qt6 programming.
.PP
The GUI description can be supplied from an environment variable
.RB ( \-\-program ),
a file
.RB ( \-\-file ),
or standard input
.RB ( \-\-stdin ).
.PP
When the dialog exits (for example when an action triggers EXIT),
.B qt6sermo
writes the current value of each named widget to standard output in the form:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
WIDGET_NAME="current_value"
.fi
.RE
.PP
which is typically captured with
.BR "eval \(dq$(qt6sermo ...)\(dq" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Enable debug mode: each character read from the dialog description is echoed
to standard output as it is fed to the lexer.
.TP
.BI \-p " VARIABLE" "\fR,\fP \-\-program=" VARIABLE
Read the XML dialog description from the environment variable
.IR VARIABLE .
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-file=" FILE
Read the XML dialog description from
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Read the XML dialog description from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-\-do= CMD
After the dialog has closed, run the shell command
.IR CMD .
The widget values are exported as environment variables first, so
.I CMD
can read them directly. The command goes through the same path as every other
command the program runs: a direct
.BR exec ()
without a shell when
.I CMD
carries no shell metacharacter, a logged fallback to the shell
.RB ( SERMO_SHELL ,
or
.B /bin/sh
by default) when it does, and a clean refusal when
.B SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK=1
is set.
.TP
.BI \-i " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-include=" FILE
Make the shell functions of
.I FILE
available to every action: each command becomes
.RB \(dq ". \fIFILE\fP; \fIcommand\fP" \(dq.
A relative
.I FILE
is resolved against the directory the program was started from.
.B \-\-do
is the exception: its command is not prefixed.
.TP
.BI \-e " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-event\-driven=" FILE
Event-driven program. The shell first reads
.I FILE
.RB ( ". \fIFILE\fP" ),
then qt6sermo runs again with
.BI \-\-include= FILE\fR,
keeping
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-c ,
.B \-\-print-ir
and
.BR \-\-program .
So
.I FILE
can set the variable that holds the dialog and define the shell functions its
actions call.
.TP
.BI \-g " FILE" "\fR,\fP \-\-glade\-xml=" FILE
Refused: only gtk3sermo and gtk4sermo load GtkBuilder (Glade) interface files.
The program ends with status 1 and says so.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-no\-warning
Suppress non-fatal warning messages.
.TP
.BI \-G " GEOMETRY" "\fR,\fP \-\-geometry=" GEOMETRY
Set the initial window placement and size,
.RI [ W x H ][ + X + Y ]
following the X11 convention (e.g. \fB800x600+0+0\fR).
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-center
Centre the windows on the screen.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-ir
Parse the GUI description, print the internal representation, and exit
without displaying any window.
.TP
.BI \-\-render\-png= FILE
Draw the dialog off screen, write it to the PNG image
.IR FILE ,
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-space\-expand= STATE
Set the default
.I expand
packing state for all widgets
.RB ( true " / " false ).
.TP
.BI \-\-space\-fill= STATE
Set the default
.I fill
packing state for all widgets
.RB ( true " / " false ).
.SH SOURCES AND TARGETS
.nh
Some tags read a device or a service instead of running a command. The text of
the tag is an all\-in\-one address:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
<input modbus>tcp://plc:502?esclave=1&registre=1</input>
<input opcua>opc.tcp://server:4840?noeud=ns=1;s=pieces</input>
<input mqtt>mqtt://broker:1883?sujet=maison/salon/temperature</input>
<input mavlink>udp://:14550?champ=batterie</input>
<input media>media://lounge?donnee=etat</input>
<action mqtt>mqtt://broker?sujet=maison/salon/lampe&valeur=on</action>
<action mavlink>tcp://127.0.0.1:5760?ordre=rtl</action>
<action media>media://lounge?commande=pause</action>
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
An
.RB < input >
of this kind only reads: nothing is written to the device.
.IP \(bu 2
.RB < "action mqtt" >,
.RB < "action mavlink" >
and
.RB < "action media" >
are the only writes. They are allowed only in a
.RB < button >
or a
.RB < menuitem >
(a write needs a click); anywhere else the dialog is refused when it is read.
A command that names a
.B sermo\-cible\-
program by hand is refused too.
.IP \(bu 2
The tag becomes a command that runs a helper program,
.BI /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-source\- PROTOCOL
(reading) or
.BI /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-cible\- PROTOCOL
(writing). The address is passed as one argument, percent\-encoded, never
through the shell.
.B SERMO_SOURCE_DIR
changes the folder; it must be an absolute path. With
.BR SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS ,
name the helper (for example
.BR /usr/libexec/sermo/sermo\-source\-modbus ).
.IP \(bu 2
Each helper comes in its own package:
.BR sermo\-source\-modbus ,
.BR sermo\-source\-opcua ,
.BR sermo\-source\-mqtt ,
.BR sermo\-source\-mavlink ,
.BR sermo\-source\-media ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-mqtt ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-mavlink ,
.BR sermo\-cible\-media .
.IP \(bu 2
MQTT and MAVLink writes go only to what an allow\-list names:
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mqtt
or
.IR /etc/sermo/cibles\-mqtt ,
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mavlink
or
.IR /etc/sermo/cibles\-mavlink .
Without a list, nothing is sent.
.IP \(bu 2
MQTT: credentials
.RB ( identifiants= )
on
.B mqtt://
(not encrypted) are refused before any connection, unless the address carries
.BR clair=oui .
.B mqtts://
requires
.B confiance=
(the authority that signed the server certificate).
.IP \(bu 2
MAVLink: telemetry; a click sets one parameter while the drone is disarmed,
gives a safety order
.RB ( rtl ", " land ,
or
.B terminaison
to cut the motors, which needs a second press within 5 s), or sends a mission
that is never started. No piloting: no arming, takeoff, mode change or
movement.
.IP \(bu 2
Media: sound and video played by mpv. A network source plays only inside
.BR bwrap ;
a local file plays inside
.B bwrap
when it is installed, and otherwise without a sandbox (a message says so).
.PP
Details (every parameter, every refusal): the user manual,
.B MANUEL_UTILISATEUR.en.md
section 7.3, in the sources at
.IR https://gitlab.com/haplo\-dialog/sermo .
.hy
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The GUI description is read from the environment variable named by
.BR \-\-program .
Widget values are printed to standard output as
.B NAME="value"
assignments when the dialog exits.
.TP
.B SERMO_DARK
Force the dark or the light variant: a value that starts with
.B 0
gives the light one, any other non\-empty value the dark one. Otherwise
qt6sermo follows the desktop theme.
.TP
.B SERMO_INPUT_MAX
The most bytes one
.RB < input >
reads, from a command or a file: 16 MiB (16777216) by default,
.B 0
for no limit. Beyond it, reading stops as if at end of file, and one warning is
printed on standard error, never on standard output. A value that is not a
number of bytes is reported, and the default applies. The progress bar is not
capped: it keeps one line at a time.
.TP
.B SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK
When set, refuse the shell fallback (see
.BR SERMO_SHELL )
used for
.RB < input >/< action >
commands that contain shell metacharacters, and fail closed instead. Commands
without shell metacharacters still run via direct
.BR exec ().
.TP
.B SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS
A comma-separated list of commands the program is allowed to run (e.g.
.BR SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS=ls,cat,/usr/local/bin/tool ).
Unset by default. Meant for deploying a dialog into a less-trusted context (a
kiosk, a guest session). An entry without a slash allows that name and the path
.B PATH
resolves it to; an entry with a slash allows exactly that path. A program that
bears an allowed name but lives elsewhere is refused. While set, the shell
fallback is refused as well. The tags of SOURCES AND TARGETS run a helper
program: name it in the list.
.IP
The 1.x names
.B HAPLO_ALLOWED_CMDS
and
.B HAPLO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK
are still read, with a warning, when the
.B SERMO_
name is not set.
.TP
.B SERMO_SHELL
The shell used for the fallback: the absolute path of an executable program
(for example
.BR SERMO_SHELL=/bin/bash ).
Unset or empty: the default shell,
.B /bin/sh
(under Windows,
.B %ComSpec%
or else
.BR cmd.exe ).
A relative path, or a file that cannot be run, is reported once on standard
error and the default shell is used.
.TP
.B SERMO_SOURCE_DIR
The folder of the helper programs that
.RB < "input modbus" >,
.RB < "input mqtt" >,
.RB < "action mqtt" >...
run (see SOURCES AND TARGETS):
.B /usr/libexec/sermo
by default. It must be an absolute path made of safe characters (letters,
digits and
.BR ._:/=,+@\- );
otherwise the dialog is refused when it is read.
.TP
.B MAIN_DIALOG
Fallback source variable, read when none of
.BR \-\-program ,
.BR \-\-file
or
.B \-\-stdin
is given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/sermo\-backend\-qt6/
Copyright and Debian changelog of the package.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/sermo\-doc/examples/
Example scripts (package
.BR sermo\-doc ).
.TP
.I /usr/libexec/sermo/
The helper programs of SOURCES AND TARGETS
.RB ( sermo\-source\-* ", " sermo\-cible\-* ),
one package each.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mqtt\fR, \fI/etc/sermo/cibles\-mqtt
The allow\-list of
.RB < "action mqtt" >:
yours (mode 600) and root's (mode 644). Without a list, nothing is sent.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sermo/cibles\-mavlink\fR, \fI/etc/sermo/cibles\-mavlink
The allow\-list of
.RB < "action mavlink" >,
same rules.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/qt6sermo.1.gz
This manual page.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Simple yes/no dialog
.nf
export DIALOG='
<window title="Confirm">
  <vbox>
    <text><label>Proceed?</label></text>
    <hbox>
      <button ok></button>
      <button cancel></button>
    </hbox>
  </vbox>
</window>'
qt6sermo --program=DIALOG
.fi
.SS Read entry value
.nf
export DIALOG='
<window>
  <vbox>
    <entry><variable>NAME</variable></entry>
    <button ok></button>
  </vbox>
</window>'
eval "$(qt6sermo --program=DIALOG)"
echo "Name: $NAME"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR haplo\-dialog\-xml (5),
the reference of the XML language, in the
.B sermo\-backend\-gtk3
package;
.BR sermo (1),
.BR gtk3sermo (1),
.BR gtk4sermo (1),
.BR gtkdialog (1)
.PP
The full manual, with examples, is installed by the package:
.IP
.B info qt6sermo
